**What worked?**  
* The part of the current policy that *detects the height condition* and **stops** (`action = 1`) once the goal is reached is essential – without it the episode would continue to accumulate –1 penalties.  
* Using the sign of the angular velocities (`v1`, `v2`) to decide the torque direction is also useful, but the thresholds (`±0.5`, `±0.2`) are too small when the links are far from the upright position; the controller flips back and forth and wastes steps.  
* When the first link is already pointing upward (`c1 ≤ 0`), the policy only looks at `v2` and `s2`. In practice the swing‑up of the *second* link is dominated by the *relative* angle `θ2` and its velocity `v2`, so a more aggressive “energy‑pump” is needed.

**What didn’t work?**  
* The rule `IF c1 > 0 THEN …` bases the torque on `v1` only, ignoring the contribution of `θ2`. When the first link is still hanging down, the second link can be used to inject energy into the system (the classic “double‑pendulum swing‑up”).  
* The “small‑zone” thresholds (`abs(v2) > 0.2`, `abs(s2) > 0.1`) are too conservative; the pendulum often stalls in the mid‑swing because the controller stops pumping torque too early.  
* There is no explicit *energy‑based* condition that tells the controller “keep adding torque until the total mechanical energy is high enough”.

