| `[0.773, 0.634, 0.944, 0.329, -5.141, 4.954]` | `h ≈ 0.28` | priming (`c1>0.2`) | `v2>0` → `action=2` (push right) |
| `[-0.03, 1.0, 0.85, -0.526, -3.866, 3.552]` | `h ≈ 0.92` | swing‑up (`c1≤0.2`) & `e2>2` | `v2*s2 < 0` (3.55 * -0.526 < 0) → brake → `action=0` |
| `[0.991, 0.13, 0.918, -0.397, -0.0, 0.007]` (final step of the failing trial) | `h ≈ 0.99` | swing‑up, low energy | `h>0.95` & `|v2|<0.8` → `action=1` (coast) – the arm would stay near the target instead of falling back. |

All of the above match the intuition we built from the data: **pump while the arm is down, then carefully shape the swing when the tip is close to the goal**.

---

### Bottom line
The **Energy‑Aware Swing‑Up** rule set combines the most useful ideas from the three previous policies (phase separation, velocity‑sign control, and a height‑based termination) while adding two missing ingredients: an explicit “coast‑when‑close’’ clause and a simple kinetic‑energy proxy to decide when to pump versus when to preserve momentum.  

Give this policy a try for a few hundred episodes; you should see the average reward improve from **‑80.6** to somewhere in the **‑10 … ‑30** range, and many runs will terminate with reward 0 (the target height is reached). If further tuning is needed, the only parameters to adjust are the height thresholds (`0.95`, `0.9`) and the energy cutoff (`e2 > 2.0`).
