**1.  What the data tell us**

| Strategy | How it was written | Mean episode‑reward (10 trials) | What we observed |
|----------|-------------------|--------------------------------|-------------------|
| **A – “full‑state pump‑brake”** (the long IF‑ELSE chain you posted first) | Uses the tip‑height *h* to decide when to coast, then tries to “prime” the first link while it is still pointing down and later “pump / brake” the second joint based on the sign of `v2·s2`. | **‑500** (all trials hit the 500‑step penalty) | The controller gets stuck in a limit‑cycle: once the first link passes the vertical it never builds enough energy to push the tip over the height‑threshold. The `abs(v2) < 0.2` gate is far too strict – the swing‑up never receives a strong enough push. |
| **B – “energy‑threshold + bias on v1”** (the second block you tried) | Adds a crude kinetic‑energy test (`0.5*v2² > 2`) and uses the velocity of the *first* joint (`v1`) as a bias when the second joint is already moving toward the upright. | **‑105.4** | This is the *best* of the three attempts. The extra energy‑check lets the controller keep pumping while the swing still has enough kinetic energy, and the bias on `v1` helps to keep the whole chain rotating. However the thresholds (`2.0` J, `0.5` for `v5`) are still arbitrary and the “coast‑near‑goal” condition never fires because the tip height never reaches `0.95` before the episode ends. |
| **C – “simple‑pump‑brake on v2 & s2”** (the “best‑so‑far” rule) | Uses only the sign of `v2` (and a tiny dead‑zone) to decide torque while the first link is still down; once the first link is up it pumps whenever `|v2|>0.2` and otherwise uses the sign of `s2`. | **‑80.6** | This is the *overall winner*. It is the most aggressive at the moment the first link is already upward, which is exactly when the tip‑height can increase quickly. The dead‑zone (`|v2|>0.2`) is small enough to let the controller keep adding energy, and the final “use s2” rule correctly decides which way to push when the swing is almost still. The main weakness is that it completely ignores `v1` (the motion of the first link) and it does not explicitly try to “coast” when the height is already sufficient. |

**Take‑away:**  
* The goal‑check (`‑c1‑(c1*c2‑s1*s2) > 1.0`) is correct in every version and should stay at the very top of the rule list.  
* The “coast‑near‑goal” rule is useful, but the height threshold has to be *lower* (e.g. `>0.7`) because the swing‑up often overshoots the exact `1.0` condition only for a single time step.  
* The best performing policies all **pump** when the second joint is moving **away** from the upright (i.e. `v2·s2 > 0`) and **brake** when it is moving **toward** the upright (`v2·s2 < 0`).  
* Adding a *soft* energy‑budget (e.g. “if kinetic energy of joint 2 is below a target, keep pumping; otherwise start braking”) makes the controller more robust.  
* The velocity of the first joint (`v1`) is a cheap source of extra energy – when the first link is already swinging upward (`c1 ≤ 0`) we can bias the torque in the same direction as `v1` to keep the whole chain rotating.

