## 2. New strategy – “Kick‑start + Velocity‑aware swing‑up”

Below is a compact rule‑set that incorporates the lessons above.  
All rules are **purely reactive** (they only use the current observation) – no history is needed.

```python
# -------------------------------------------------
#  Helper values (same notation as before)
c1 = obs[0]          # cos(theta1)
s1 = obs[1]          # sin(theta1)
c2 = obs[2]          # cos(theta2)
s2 = obs[3]          # sin(theta2)
v1 = obs[4]          # angular velocity of joint 1
v2 = obs[5]          # angular velocity of joint 2

# tip height indicator (same as before)
h = -c1 - (c1*c2 - s1*s2)   # = -cos(theta1) - cos(theta1+theta2)
# -------------------------------------------------
# 1. Goal / near‑goal handling
if h > 1.0:                     # already above the target
    action = 1                  # coast – episode will terminate
elif h > 0.92 and abs(v2) < 0.4:
    action = 1                  # coast – we are close enough, keep energy
# -------------------------------------------------
# 2. Priming phase – first link still below the pivot (c1 > 0)
elif c1 > 0.0:
    # (a) strong outward motion – keep pushing outward
    if v2 > 0.25:
        action = 2
    elif v2 < -0.25:
        action = 0
    # (b) first joint already moving upward – help it
    elif v1 > 0.5:               # first link climbing fast
        action = 2 if s2 >= 0 else 0
    elif v1 < -0.5:              # first link moving down fast
        action = 0 if s2 >= 0 else 2
    # (c) no clear velocity – use geometry to decide a small kick
    else:
        if s2 > 0.1:              # second link pointing to the right
            action = 2
        elif s2 < -0.1:           # second link pointing to the left
            action = 0
        else:                     # truly dead‑locked – give a tiny nudge
            action = 2 if v1 >= 0 else 0
# -------------------------------------------------
# 3. Swing‑up / balancing phase – first link already above vertical (c1 <= 0)
else:
    # (a) fast enough motion – treat it as the “outer/inner’’ regime
    if abs(v2) > 0.30:
        # outward motion (v2 * s2 > 0) → reinforce
        if v2 * s2 > 0:
            action = 2 if v2 > 0 else 0
        # inward motion (v2 * s2 < 0) → brake
        else:
            action = 0 if v2 > 0 else 2
    # (b) moderate speed – rely on sign of sin(theta2) but also on v2 sign
    elif abs(v2) > 0.10:
        # if we are moving outward but sin is small, keep torque to push a bit more
        if v2 * s2 > 0:
            action = 2 if v2 > 0 else 0
        else:
            # moving inward – apply a small opposite torque (brake)
            action = 0 if v2 > 0 else 2
    # (c) very slow – give a gentle push in the direction the link points
    else:
        if s2 > 0.05:
            action = 2
        elif s2 < -0.05:
            action = 0
        else:
            action = 1          # truly balanced, just coast
```

### Why this should be better

