## 2. New/Improved Rule Set  

Below is a **deterministic rule‑based controller** that keeps the three logical phases (goal, priming, swing‑up/balancing) but replaces the old balancing logic with a *continuous torque estimate* that is then discretised to the three actions. The design follows the classic “energy‑pump” idea for the double‑pendulum while staying within the allowed discrete action space.

```python
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Helper aliases (for readability)
c1 = obs[0]          # cos(theta1)
s1 = obs[1]          # sin(theta1)
c2 = obs[2]          # cos(theta2)   (relative angle)
s2 = obs[3]          # sin(theta2)
v1 = obs[4]          # angular velocity of joint 1
v2 = obs[5]          # angular velocity of joint 2

# tip‑height indicator (same as in all previous strategies)
h = -c1 - (c1*c2 - s1*s2)      # = -cos(theta1) - cos(theta1+theta2)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------- 1. Goal / near‑goal ----------
if h > 1.0:
    action = 1                                   # already above target → coast
elif h > 0.92 and abs(v2) < 0.30:
    action = 1                                   # almost there, stop pumping

# ---------- 2. Priming phase (first link still below vertical) ----------
elif c1 > 0.0:                                   # theta1 still > 0 (link hanging)
    # We want to give the second joint a quick push in the direction it
    # is already moving, but avoid fighting when it is already fast.
    if v2 > 0.25:
        action = 2                               # torque to the right
    elif v2 < -0.25:
        action = 0                               # torque to the left
    else:
        # No strong velocity → use the sign of s2 (the relative angle)
        if s2 > 0:
            action = 2
        elif s2 < 0:
            action = 0
        else:
            action = 1                           # perfectly aligned → coast

# ---------- 3. Swing‑up / Balancing phase (first link already above vertical) ----------
else:
    # ---- 3a. Continuous torque estimate ----
    #   • Energy‑pump term:   -k_e * (v2 * s2)   → adds energy when the second link
    #                         moves opposite to its angle (classic swing‑up).
    #   • Feed‑forward from joint‑1 velocity:   k_f * v1
    #   • Small damping term to avoid runaway oscillations:   -k_d * v2
    #   • Cosine‑bias term that accounts for the coupling of the two links:
    #       k_c * c2   (when c2 < 0 the second link points upwards, we need less torque)
    #
    # The constants were tuned by hand on a few short roll‑outs.
    k_e = 0.45          # swing‑up gain
    k_f = 0.12          # first‑joint feed‑forward
    k_d = 0.20          # damping on v2
    k_c = 0.15          # cosine bias

    torque = -k_e * (v2 * s2) + k_f * v1 - k_d * v2 + k_c * c2

    # ---- 3b. Dead‑zone / fine‑balance ----
    # When the tip is already close to the vertical (|s2| small) we want a softer
    # controller that mainly damps the motion.
    if abs(s2) < 0.10:
        torque = torque * 0.5                     # reduce aggressiveness
        # Add a tiny bias that pushes the second link towards the upright position
        torque += 0.2 * (-s2)

    # ---- 3c. Discretise to the three actions ----
    if torque > 0.25:
        action = 2          # right torque
    elif torque < -0.25:
        action = 0          # left torque
    else:
        action = 1          # no torque
```

